Skin cancer (melanoma),the first sign of a melanoma is often a new mole or a change in the appearance of an existing mole normal moles are usually round or oval with a smooth edge and no bigger than 6mm 1 4 inch in diameter speak to your gp as soon arsenic possible if you notice changes in a mole freckle or patch of skin especially if the changes happen over a few weeks or months signs to look out for include a mole that is a helpful way to tell the difference between a normal mole and a melanoma is the abcde checklist melanomas can appear anywhere on your body but they most commonly appear on the back legs arms and face they may sometimes develop underneath a nail in rare cases melanoma can develop in the eye noticing a dark spot or changes in vision can be signs although it is more likely to be diagnose during a routine eye examination read further information,,,
