Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD),ocd affects people in different ways it usually causes a particular pattern of idea and behaviour this pattern has 4 main steps almost everyone has unpleasant or unwanted thoughts at some point in their life this might be a concern that you ve forgotten to lock the door or that you might get a disease from touching other people it could be sudden unwelcome violent or offensive mental images most people are able to put these types of thoughts and concerns into context they can then carry on with their day to day life they do not repeatedly think about worries they recognise as senseless or excessive if you have persistent and unwanted thoughts that dominate your thinking you may have developed an compulsion some common obsessions that affect people with ocd include compulsions happen as a way of trying to reduce or prevent the harm of the obsessive thought however this behaviour is either excessive or not connected at all for example a person who fears that they ll become contaminated with dirt and germs may wash their hands repeatedly or someone with a fear of causing harm to their family may have the urge to repeat an action many times to try to neutralise the thought of harm this type of compulsive behaviour is particularly common in children with ocd most people with ocd realise that such compulsive behaviour is irrational they might know it makes no logical sense but they cannot stop acting on their compulsion not all compulsive behaviours will be obvious to other people,,,
