Eating disorders,symptoms of an eating disorder include your relationships with those closest to you can also be affected you might also experience physical symptoms like,"Eating disorders are often blamed on the social pressure to be thin, but the causes are usually more complex. There are lots of possible reasons why a person might develop an eating disorder. The causes are different for everybody. These causes can be psychological, environmental, and biological.","It can be difficult to identify that a loved one has an eating disorder. There are warning signs you can look out for like: Read more about how to support someone with an eating disorder Someone with an eating disorder may also have: Peace Pathwayhas information and resources for autistic people with an eating disorder. If you have any other health conditions it’s important to discuss this with your healthcare professional so they can assess how this could impact your treatment. Some conditions involve food and eating, but aren’t related to concerns about weight or body shape. These includeavoidant restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID). ARFID causes a person to avoid certain foods or types of food, restrict how much they eat, or both. Learn more about ARFID on the Beat website","An eating disorder can have a major impact on your life. It can affect your relationships, job, schoolwork, and physical health. With all eating disorders, the first step is recognising that there’s a problem and being willing to seek help. If you’re worried that you have an eating disorder, see a GP as soon as you can. It can be very hard to admit that you have a problem and ask for help. Many people feel scared about asking for help but its important to get this help as soon as possible. It might help to bring a friend or loved one with you to your appointment. Your loved one might not know they have an eating disorder. They might deny it or avoid talking about their eating or their weight because it’s such a painful stressful subject for them. Let them know you’re worried about them and encourage them to see a GP. You could even offer to go with them. Read more about supporting someone with an eating disorder There’s a lot of information and advice available for parents and carers, including: Treating an eating disorder usually involves monitoring physical health and supporting mental health. The type of treatment used depends on: For some people, self-help or guided self-help can be effective. When guided self-help isn’t appropriate, or wouldn’t be effective enough on its own, a common treatment ispsychological therapy. This may be working individually, in a group or as a family. Specialist dietitians may also be part of your treatment. Your physical health may also be monitored to support your recovery. For some people it can be helpful to prescribe medication in combination with psychological treatment. Medication alone isn’t usually effective for eating disorders. Read about the SIGN clinical guidelines for treatment of an eating disorder in Scotland"
