Chickenpox,chickenpox causes a rash of red itchy spots that turn into fluid filledblisters they then crust over to form scabs which eventually drop off the spots normally appear in clusters and tend to be but the spots can be anywhere on the body including the rash starts as small antsy red spots these develop ablisteron top and become very itchy after about 12 to 14 hours after a day or two the fluid in the blisters gets cloudy and they begin to dry out and crust over after 1 to 2 weeks the crusting skin will fall off naturally new spots can keep appearing in wave for 3 to 5 days after the rash begins so different groups of spots may be at different stages of blistering or drying out before the rash appears you or your child may have some mildflu like symptoms including these symptoms especially the fever tend to be more common and worse in adults than in children for most children chickenpox is a mild illness that gets better on its own chickenpox tends to be more severe in adults than children adults have a higher risk of developing complications,,,
Sjogren’s syndrome,the most commonly reported symptoms of sj gren s syndrome are a dry mouth and eyes which can lead to other associated symptoms however these symptoms can be common in old age and most people with dry eyes or a dry mouth do not have sj gren s syndrome many women also experience vaginal dryness which can make sex painful adry mouthcan lead to dry eyes can lead to these symptoms can get worse when you re in more serious cases of sj gren s syndrome the immune system can attack other parts of the body causing symptoms and conditions such as several soaps and creams are specifically designed for people with dry skin your pharmacist or gp can advise you vaginal dryness can be treated using a lubricant some women also use oestrogen creams orhormone replacement therapy hrt muscle and joint pains can be treat with an over the counternon steroidal anti inflammatory drug nsaid such asibuprofen if this doesn t work see your gp as stronger nsaids are available on prescription nsaids can increase your risk of developingstomach ulcersand internal bleeding particularly if they re taken on a long term basis if you find swallowing nsaids difficult because of your dry mouth you can try an nsaid cream that s rubbed into affected joints nsaids aren t recommended for pregnant or breastfeed women or for people with pre existing risk factors for cardiovascular or kidney conditions,,,
