Peripheral neuropathy,symptoms of peripheral neuropathy can change depending on which type you have they can also develop quickly or slowly the symptoms of mononeuropathy can vary because they depend on which specific nerve has been affected example symptoms could include even if the cause of your peripheral neuropathy cannot be treated you can get help with managing your symptoms if you re in pain you may be prescribed different types of medication this is because standard analgesic like ibuprofen and paracetamol are often not effective at treating nerve pain if you re experiencing pain in a particular area of your body capsaicin cream may help capsaicin is the substance that makes chilli peppers hot it s thought to stop the nerves sending pain messages to the brain making it useful for nerve pain menthol cream can have a similar effect if your pain only affects a small area of skin you may be offered a lidocaine plaster this is a large sticking plaster that contains a local anaesthetic which is absorbed into the skin the plaster covers if topical measures do not work your gp may consider a neuropathic agent these include some types of anti depressants if you re struggling with muscle weakness you may benefit from physiotherapy it can help improve weakness occupational therapycan help you find new ways of completing difficult tasks if you have foot drop or tripping orthotics can help with ankle supports if you re experiencing hyperhidrosis excessive sweating you may be offered botulinum toxin botox injections these could help reduce your sweating if you re struggling to empty your bladder you may be offered urodynamic studies depending on the result you may be offered medication orurinary catheterisationto help you,,,
Postnatal depression,postnatal depression affects everyone differently it can start at any point in the first year after giving birth it may develop suddenly or gradually it can also continue from depression during pregnancy many people feel a bit down tearful or anxious in the first week after giving birth this is often called the baby blues and doesn t last for more than 2 weeks after giving birth if your symptoms last longer or start later you could have postnatal depression the main symptoms of postnatal depression include these symptoms can affect your day to day life and your relationships with your baby family and friends,"The cause of postnatal depression isn’t completely clear. It has been linked to: Even if there’s no specific cause of your symptoms, having a baby is a life-changing event that can sometimes trigger depression. It often takes time to adapt to becoming a new parent. Looking after a baby can be stressful and exhausting.",,"Postnatal depression can be lonely, distressing and frightening. But, support and treatments are available. With the right treatment and support, most people make a full recovery, although it can take time. Talk to your GP practice about different treatment options so you can decide together what’s best for you. Your GP might also check your physical health to see if there are any problems that may need to be addressed. For example, you may be anaemic after giving birth. This could add to any feelings of depression you might have. Psychological therapies are usually the first treatment recommended for postnatal depression. Guided self-help involves working through a book or an online course. You can do this on your own or with some help from a therapist. It focusses on the issues you might be facing, with practical advice on how to deal with them. The courses usually last 9 to 12 weeks. Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) is a type of talking therapy. It’s based on the idea that unhelpful and unrealistic thinking leads to negative behaviour. CBT aims to break this cycle and find new ways of thinking that can help you behave in a more positive way. CBT can be 1-to-1 with a therapist or in a group. Treatment will often last 3 to 4 months. Interpersonal therapy (IPT) involves talking to a therapist about the problems you’re experiencing. It aims to identify problems in your relationships with family, friends or partners. It then looks at how they might relate to your feelings of depression. Treatment usually lasts 3 to 4 months. Antidepressantsmay be recommended if: There are many things you can try yourself to improve your symptoms and help you cope. You could also try to: Try to avoid Ask your health visitor about support services in your area. They may be able to put you in touch with a social worker, counsellor or local support group. It can be reassuring to meet other people who are going through something similar. Find postnatal depression support and information in your area You might be referred to aspecialist perinatal mental health teamif: Your healthcare team will discuss if there are other treatments that are suitable for you. Sometimes, for a small number of people, coming into hospital can help speed up recovery. If your doctor or nurse suggests this, then you can be offered a place in aMother and Baby Unit. This means you can continue to look after your baby while you get the best treatment for your illness."
