Polymyalgia rheumatica,polymyalgia rheumatica often causes pain and stiffness this can affect the shoulders neck and hips these symptoms can develop quickly over a few days or weeks both sides of the body are usually affected the stiffness often feels worse first thing in the morning after you wake up it usually starts to improve after about 45 minutes as you become more active polymyalgia rheumatica may also cause if you have been diagnosed with polymyalgia rheumatica and you suddenly develop vision loss or changes in your vision like double vision contact an optician immediately or phone 111,"The cause of polymyalgia rheumatica is unknown. But, genetic and environmental factors can influence it. Polymyalgia rheumatica is also age-related. Most people who are diagnosed with the condition are over 70. It’s very rare in people younger than 50. Polymyalgia rheumatic is more common in women than men.",,"The main treatment for polymyalgia rheumatica is acorticosteroidmedication called prednisolone. This comes as a pill that’s prescribed by your GP. Prednisolone blocks certain chemicals that cause inflammation in the body. You’ll be prescribed a higher dose of prednisolone at first. This will be slowly reduced over time. This is likely to be over a year. Sometimes you may be prescribed other medicines with corticosteroids. This can help to prevent relapses or it may allow your dose of prednisolone to be reduced. Additional medicines are usually added by rheumatology doctors. They include a weekly dose of methotrexate or a daily dose of leflunomide. Painkillers may also be recommended. These can be helpful if you also have arthritis or chronic pain. There areside effects linked to taking steroidslong-term. This means your healthcare professional may also prescribe medication to protect your bones. For example, calcium and vitamin D supplements and bisphosphonates. You may also be prescribed proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) to protect your stomach."
