Rheumatoid arthritis,the symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis vary from person to person they can come and go and may change over time symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis that affect the joints include in some people firm swellings called rheumatoid nodules also develop under the skin around affected joints some people with rheumatoid arthritis can experience other symptoms this includes,"Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune condition. This means it’s caused by the body’s immune system attacking itself. The immune system helps to defend our body from infections. But, in rheumatoid arthritis the immune system sends antibodies to the lining of the joints by mistake. The antibodies then attack the tissues surrounding the joint. This causes the thin layer of cells (synovium) covering the joints to become sore and inflamed. Inflammation also causes chemicals to be released. These chemicals thicken the synovium and damage the: The chemicals can also change the shape of the joint over time.",,"There’s no cure for rheumatoid arthritis but with treatment the joint pain, swelling and stiffness will improve and the risk of joint damage can be reduced. Different types of medications can be used to treat rheumatoid arthritis to relieve joint pain, stiffness and swelling. This will also reduce the chance of developing joint damage. The two main types of medication are: They usually need to be taken for a long-time. They are most effective when started early after the first symptoms. You may also need other medication to relieve the joint pain and stiffness.. For example: You may be offered other treatments to help your rheumatoid arthritis symptoms. This includes: If you have rheumatoid arthritis, surgery is sometimes needed to: Surgery for rheumatoid arthritis includes: Some people with rheumatoid arthritis try complementary therapies like:"
