Peripheral neuropathy,symptoms of peripheral neuropathy can vary depending on which type you have they can also develop quickly or slowly the symptoms of mononeuropathy can vary because they depend on which specific nerve has been affected example symptoms could include even if the cause of your peripheral neuropathy cannot be treated you can get help with managing your symptom if you re in pain you may be prescribed different types of medication this is because standard painkillers like ibuprofen and paracetamol are often not effective at treating nerve pain if you re experiencing pain in a particular area of your body capsaicin cream may help capsaicin is the substance that makes chilli peppers hot it s thought to stop the nerves sending pain messages to the brain making it useful for nerve pain menthol cream can have a similar effect if your pain only affects a small area of skin you may be offered a lidocaine plaster this is a large sticking plaster that contains a local anaesthetic which is absorbed into the skin the plaster covers if topical measure do not work your gp may consider a neuropathic agent these include some types of anti depressants if you re struggling with muscle weakness you may benefit from physiotherapy it can help improve weakness occupational therapycan help you find new ways of completing difficult tasks if you have foot drop or tripping orthotics can help with ankle supports if you re experiencing hyperhidrosis excessive sweating you may be offered botulinum toxin botox injections these could help reduce your sweating if you re struggling to empty your bladder you may be offered urodynamic studies depending on the result you may be offered medication orurinary catheterisationto help you,,,
Rosacea,rosacea causes many symptoms although not everyone will experience them all rosacea can cause redness blushing on the face chin and chest these areas may also feel hot warm or painful the redness usually comes and goes and lasts a few minutes each time if rosacea gets worse the skin on your cheeks nose and forehead may be red all the time other symptoms of rosacea include there may be times when your rosacea symptoms get better or worse the following images were sourced fromdermentnz org,,,"There’s currently no cure for rosacea, but treatment can help control the symptoms. It may get worse if it isn’t treated. To help treat rosacea, your GP may suggest: A GP may refer you to a skin specialist, known as a dermatologist, if treatments don’t work."
