Ankylosing spondylitis,the symptoms of ankylosing spondylitis can vary from person to person they can develop slowly over several months or years the main symptoms include,"It’s not known exactly what causes ankylosing spondylitis. Studies have shown there’s a link between it and a gene known as HLA-B27. Having this gene does not necessarily mean you will develop ankylosing spondylitis. It’s estimated that 8% of people carry the HLA-B27 gene, but most do not have ankylosing spondylitis.",,"There is no cure for ankylosing spondylitis. But, treatment is available to help relieve the symptoms. Treatment can also help delay or prevent the spine from fusing (joining up) and stiffening. In most cases, treatment will involve a mix of: Most people with ankylosing spondylitis will not need surgery. But, if the joint becomes very damaged, joint replacement surgery may be recommended. This can help to improve pain and movement in the joint. Physiotherapy is a key part of treating ankylosing spondylitis. A Physiotherapist can explain the best exercises for you. They can also draw up an exercise programme that suits you. Types of physiotherapy for ankylosing spondylitis include: With hydrotherapy the buoyancy of the water helps make movement easier by supporting you. The warmth can also help relax your muscles. Some people prefer to swim or play sport to keep flexible. This is usually fine, although some daily stretching and exercise is also important. Speak to your Physiotherapist or Rheumatologist if you’re worried about taking up a new type of sport or exercise. The National Ankylosing Spondylitis Society (NASS) has information aboutdifferent types of exercise to help you manage your condition."
