Acute myeloid leukaemia: Children,as the leukaemia cells multiply in the bone marrow the production of normal blood cells is reduced children may therefore become tired and lethargic because of anaemia which is caused by a lack of red blood cells children may develop bruises and bleeding may take longer to stop because of the low number of platelets present in their blood sometimes they may suffer from infections because of low numbers of normal white blood cell a child is likely to feel generally unwell and may complain of aches and pains in the limbs or may have swollen lymph glands at first the symptoms are just like those of angstrom viral infection but when they continue for more than a week or two the diagnosis usually becomes clear,,,
