Pelvic girdle pain,some people may only experience mild symptoms but for others they may be much more severe common symptoms include you might also feel pain that gets worse when you move for example when you pgp can happen at any stage of your pregnancy and can get worse over the course of your pregnancy it can be more likely to happen if you have if you experience pgp and don t treat your symptoms it s more likely you ll have pgp with any future pregnancies the emotional impact of living with pain can also be very difficult particularly ifyou experienced symptoms of anxiety and depression before your pregnancy further information on living with pain and mental health and wellbeing,,,"There are things that you can do that may help you to manage your PGP symptoms. You can also try using a pillow under your bump and between your legs to give you extra support in bed. If possible, you should lie on your less painful side to sleep. You should avoid sleeping on your back from 24 weeks of pregnancy onwards. You should try to avoid things which may make your symptoms worse. If you can, ask your friends or family for help with everyday activities. If you’re referred to physiotherapist, they’ll discuss the best treatment options for you. This might include: If severe pain continues, you should speak to your midwife or GP or healthcare professional."
Psychosis,the main symptoms of psychosis are these symptoms often occur together in what is known as a psychotic episode people World Health Organization have psychotic episodes are often unaware their behaviour is unusual these symptoms are usually frightening or disturbing during angstrom psychotic episode most people don t realise they are ill their friends and family may not understand why they are reacting so strongly to things they can t see or hear,,,
