Type 1 diabetes,if your blood sugar is too high you may the symptoms of type 1 diabetes usually develop very quickly in young people this is normally over a few hours or days in adults the symptoms often take longer to develop over a few days or weeks the symptoms of type 1 diabetes should disappear when you start taking insulin and you bring your blood sugars closer to normal levels read more about hyperglycaemia,"Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition. Your normal immune system mistakes the cells in your pancreas as harmful. It then attacks them. Without insulin, your body will break down its own fat and muscle, resulting in weight loss. This can lead to a serious short-term condition called diabetic ketoacidosis. Insulin is also needed to move glucose out of the blood and into your cells to be used for energy.",,"It’s important that diabetes is diagnosed as early as possible. If left untreated, type 1 diabetes is a life-threatening condition. It’s essential that treatment is started early. Type 1 diabetes can’t be cured at the moment. Treatment aims to keep your blood glucose levels as normal as possible. This will help control your symptoms. It’ll also help to prevent health problems developing later in life. If you’re diagnosed with type 1 diabetes, you’ll be referred to a diabetes care team. That team will provide specialist treatment and monitoring. You’ll need regular insulin injections or insulin pump to keep your glucose levels normal. You’ll be helped to understand how to do this. You’ll also learn how to match the insulin you inject to the food (carbohydrate) you eat. You’ll also take into account your: Insulin injections come in several different forms. Each one works slightly differently. You’ll most likely need a combination of different insulin preparations. Insulin is given to some patients by a by an insulin pump. This means you’ll get a programmed flow of insulin. This will either be controlled by you or by glucose monitoring values. Both of these methods replace long-acting insulin injections. There are alternatives to insulin injections and pumps. They’re only suitable for a very small number of patients. This is because they need very strong drugs to suppress your immune system. They alternatives to insulin injections and pumps are:"
